<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baptist War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Christmas Rebellion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baptist War, or Christmas Rebellion, was a rising of up to 60,000 enslaved people in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> that began on 25 December 1831. Led by the Baptist deacon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>, it started as a strike for wages and turned into an armed revolt in which many plantations were burned. It was crushed by early 1832 and hundreds were executed, but the reaction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> Abolition Act of 1833.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baptist-war.jpg" alt="Baptist War" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adolphe Duperly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duperly_(1833)_Destruction_of_the_Roehampton_Estate_January_1832.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
